('class6-math-en-chunk-1-018','class6-math-en','class6-math-en-ch1','en',36,39,41,44,18,'is 167 kg. If 4 of the bucket 1 contains water weighing 7 kg, determine the weight of the empty bucket. 1 1 9. Show that, the product of the fractions 5— and a5 is equal to the product of their L.C.M. and H.C.M. Simplify (from 10 to 15): 10. 2 of 423 of 2-155 8 5 4 10 2 9 2026 2026 Mathematics 37 i. {3t+24«11)+(34+21 of it 2 2 2 2 2 2 12, 120 x) 42 +313 of 5442-3 23 16 8 2 \7 14 2 5 1 4 1 7 1 2 2 13. —x}—x | 3—+8—]|+] 6—-3—|$+3—-+4—x4— 5 32 3 9 12 8 7 5 3 1 1 3 17/2 1 421 14. 7——-|3—+4—-—| —-—+— 2 4 4 3\3 6 8 5 1 3 2 1 1 1 3 15. 1—+7——]1—+43—-—] 6—-—2—of 1-—+— 6 3 4 3 2 3 2 4 Decimal Fraction 1-20 Addition of decimal fractions 10-5, 2-08 and 16-745 are three decimal fractions. In 16-745 there is the digit ‘5’ in the position of the thousandth. There is no digit in the place of thousandth in the number 2-08. 10-500 2-080 16-745 29-325 .. In case of addition of decimal numbers we need to arrange them in such a way that the decimal points remain one below the other according to their position. Example 1. Add : 33-01 + 3-7 + 14-85 Solution: 33-01 3-70 14-85 51-56 38 Mathematics Alternative method : 33-01 + 3-7 + 14-85 _ 3301 n 37 n 1485 3301+370+ 1485 ~ 100 10 100 100 = 5156 _ 51.56 100 1-21 Subtraction of decimal fractions Similar to the case of addition of decimal fractions, we need to arrange the given fractions keeping the decimal points directly one below another and then we have to subtract them. Example 2. Subtract 1-71 from 23-657. Solution : By arranging the given numbers keeping the decimal points one below another according to their position , we get 23-657 1-710 21-947 1-22 Multiplication of decimal fractions Example 3. Multiply 0.0657 by 0-75. Solution : 657 75 3285 45990 49275 . 0-0657x0-75 = 0-049275 Observe > Firstly, the decimal points are avoided and the numbers are multiplied as in the ordinary multiplication of numbers. After omitting the decimal point from the multiplicand, the zero on its extreme left has been omitted. 2026 2026 Mathematics > > 39 There are 4 digits inthe multiplicand and 2 digits in the multiplier after the decimal point i.e. after total (4 + 2) or 6 digits in the multiplicand and the multiplier. The product is formed by placing the decimal point on the left of 6 digits counting from the right side of the product. It requires put a 0 (zero) before the decimal point on the left of 6 digits counting from the right of the product. Alternative method : -0657 x -75 _ 657, 75 10000 100 657 x 75 — 49275 ~ 10000 x 100 1000000 = -049275 [Expressing in decimal fraction] [Expressing decimal fractions into common fractions] 1-23 Division of decimal fractions Example 4. Divide 808-9 by 25. Solution : Alternative method : 25) 808.9 ( 32-356 Solution ; 808-9 + 25 75 58 _ 808-9 808-9x4 3235-6 50 ~ 25 25x4 ~—-:100 89 = 32-356 15 140 125 150 150 0 Required quotient is 32-356.',553,'06-Mathematics.pdf','tesseract','tesseract 5.3.4 -l eng, pdftoppm 200dpi gray; verbatim OCR, whitespace collapsed only; ~50-word overlap with previous chunk in same chapter; maths notation may OCR noisily, see extraction log'),
('class6-math-en-chunk-1-019','class6-math-en','class6-math-en-ch1','en',39,41,44,46,19,'fractions] 1-23 Division of decimal fractions Example 4. Divide 808-9 by 25. Solution : Alternative method : 25) 808.9 ( 32-356 Solution ; 808-9 + 25 75 58 _ 808-9 808-9x4 3235-6 50 ~ 25 25x4 ~—-:100 89 = 32-356 15 140 125 150 150 0 Required quotient is 32-356. Observe > Division has been done on as in the case of division of integers. > The decimal point has been inserted in the quotient just after the completion of division of integral part, because the decimal part has been divided. > The process of division is performed placing 0 (zero) on the right of the remainder. 40 Mathematics 1:24 H.C.F. and L.C.M. of decimal fractions Determination of H.C.F. and L.C.M. of 2, 1-2 and 0-08 Given fractions are exactly equal to 2-00, 1-20 and 0-08 Now, H.C.F. of 200, 120 and 8 is 8 and L.C.M. = 600 .. The required H.C.F. = 0-08 and the L.C.M. = 6-00 Observe : In some cases, zeroes are put as required in the given decimal fractions in order to make the number of digits equal after the decimal point. Then the H.C.F. and the L.C.M. of the fractions are determined considering them a whole number. After that, decimal points are to be put in the formed H.C.F. and L.C.M. after as many digits from its right as there are in each of the changed decimal fractions after their decimal points. Then we will get our required H.C.F. and L.C.M. Alternative method Expressing the given numbers in their lowest common fractions, we get, 922 4.9-P_8 and p= = 2% 1 10 5 100 25 H.C.F. of numerator 2, 6 and 2 of the fraction = 2 and L.C.M.=6 and L.C.M. of denominator 1, 5 and 25 of the fractions = 25 and H.C.F. = 1 . H.C.F. of the fractions == =-08 and L.C.M= .- 6-00 Example 5. Mr. Azim sold 50 quintals of rice at Tk. 30-75 per kg., 5 quintals of onion at Tk. 2025 perkg. and 17 quintals of wheat at Tk. 17-50 per kg. He deposited an amount of Tk. 1,10,000-00 to a bank account from the fund obtained. How much will be left with him ? Solution : 1 quintals = 100 kg. .. Cost of 50 quintals of rice = Tk. (30-75 x 100 x 50) = Tk. 1,53,750-00 .. Cost of 5 quintals of onion = Tk. (2025 x 100 x 5) = Tk. 10,125-00 .. Cost of 17 quintals of wheat = Tk. (17-50 x 100 x 17) = Tk. 29,750-00 Mr. Azim got the amount in total 2026 Mathematics 4l = Tk. (1,53,7505-00 + 10,125-00 + 29,750-00) = Tk. 1,93,625-00 ..The amount left with him is Tk. (1,93,625-00 —1,10,000-00) = Tk. 83,625-00 Exercise 1-6 1. Find the sum : (a) 0-325 + 2-368 + 1-2 + 0-29 (b) 13-001 + 23-01 + 0-005 + 80-6 2. Find the difference : (a) 95-02— 2-895 (b) 3-15— 1-6758 (c) 899 — 23-987 3. Multiply : (a) -218x3 (b) -33x-02x-18 (c) 0754x1000 (d) -05x-007x-0003 4. Determine the quotient : (a) 9-75+25 — (b) 97-17 +-0123 (c) -168+-0125 5. Simplify : [3-5 {7-8-2-3-(12-75—-9-25)}] +5 6. Toma had Tk. 50. She gave her younger brother Tk. 15-50 and her friend Tk. 12:75. What amount of money was left with her ?',553,'06-Mathematics.pdf','tesseract','tesseract 5.3.4 -l eng, pdftoppm 200dpi gray; verbatim OCR, whitespace collapsed only; ~50-word overlap with previous chunk in same chapter; maths notation may OCR noisily, see extraction log'),
('class6-math-en-chunk-1-020','class6-math-en','class6-math-en-ch1','en',41,44,46,49,20,'-33x-02x-18 (c) 0754x1000 (d) -05x-007x-0003 4. Determine the quotient : (a) 9-75+25 — (b) 97-17 +-0123 (c) -168+-0125 5. Simplify : [3-5 {7-8-2-3-(12-75—-9-25)}] +5 6. Toma had Tk. 50. She gave her younger brother Tk. 15-50 and her friend Tk. 12:75. What amount of money was left with her ? 7. Parul Begum has 100 hundredths of land. She cultivated rice in 40-5 hundredths of it, Capsicum in 20-2 hundredths and potato in 10-75 hundredths. The rest of the part is used for the cultivation of brinjal. How much part of the land was used the for cultivation of brinjal? 8. 1 inch = 2-54 cm. Then, 8-5 inch =........ cm ? 9. Acar goes 45-6 km per hour. How much time will the car take to go 319.2km ? Forma-6, Mathematics, Class-6 42 Mathematics 10. A teacher bought oranges of Tk. 722-15 at the rate of Tk. 60.60 a dozen and distributed the oranges equally among 13 of his students. How many oranges will each student get? 11. 0-15 part of the total length of a bamboo is in the mud and 0-65 part is in water. If the length of the bamboo above the water is 4 m., what is the length of the whole bamboo ? 12. Abdur Rahman gave -125 part of his property to his wife. He gave -50 part of the rest of the property to his son and -25 part to his daughter. After that he noticed, he was still left with property worth Tk. 3,15,000-00. What is the total value of Abdur Rahman’s property ? Sample Question Multiple Choice Question 1. Which one of the following are co-prime pairs ? (a) 12,18 (b) 19, 38 (c) 22, 27 (d) 28, 35 2. What is the H.C.F. of 12, 18 and 48 ? (a) 3 (b) 6 (c) 8 (d) 12 3. In one digit natural numbers, there are (i) 4 prime numbers (ii) 4 composite numbers (iii) 5 odd numbers Which of the following is correct? (a)iandii (b)iandiii (c) ii andi (d) i, ii and iii 2026 2026 Mathematics 43 In the light of the following information, answer questions 4 and 5 Figure : in the square figure each rectangle is equal 4. Into how many rectangles is the square divided ? (a) 1 (b) 4 (c) 6 (d) 24 5. How much part of the square is every rectangle? (a) F pat (by part) part, (Ad) part Creative Question A post has x of its length in mud, and x in water. The part above the water is 29 meters long. (a) Express 0.15 in its lowest fractional form. (b) Determine the total length of the post. (c) Convert both fractions in the stem to like denominators, and iden- tify which part (in mud and in water) is longer. 44 Mathematics Short Answer Question 1. Write the greatest and the smallest numbers consisting of seven digits having 7 in the first and 6 in the last position of the numbers ? 2.Find the HCF (Highest Common Factor) of 12 and 30 using prime factorization. 3. What has to be added to the sum of the fractions 107, and 385) i in order to make the sum 100 ? 4.If 3 inches = 7.62 cm, find how many centimeters are in 9.5 inches. 2026',552,'06-Mathematics.pdf','tesseract','tesseract 5.3.4 -l eng, pdftoppm 200dpi gray; verbatim OCR, whitespace collapsed only; ~50-word overlap with previous chunk in same chapter; maths notation may OCR noisily, see extraction log'),
('class6-math-en-chunk-2-001','class6-math-en','class6-math-en-ch2','en',45,47,50,52,1,'2026 Chapter two Ratios and Percentages In our day to day activities we make comparisons in some way or other between many things. For example, we compare two friends regarding is taller or when dividing a cake we calculate how much of the whole cake or how many times one got more than the other. Both ratios and percentages are used to make comparisons between multiple objects easier. So it is very important to have a good understanding of ratio and percentage. There is also a relationship between percentages and fractions. This chapter will discuss these issues. At the end of the chapter, the students will be able to— > explain what ratio is. > solve the problems related to simple ratio. > express percentage to fraction and fraction to percentage. > express ratio to percentage and percentage to ratio. > describe unitary method and the method of percentage accounting; and > solve the arithmetic problems about time and work, time and food, time and distance with the help of unitary method and the method of percentage accounting. 2-1 Ratio In our everyday life we often compare two things of the same kind. For example, if the height of Nabil is 150 cm and that of his sister is 140 cm, we can say that the height of Nabil is (150—140) cm or 10 cm more than that of his sister. Thus we can make comparison by finding out the difference. 46 Mathematics Again, if we want to compare the areas of two squares, comparing by differences of area is not justified. Rather, we can make justified comparison of the areas of two squares by finding out how many times a square is greater or smaller than the other. We can make this comparison by dividing the area of a square by that of the other. This comparison with division is called ratio. ‘ : ’ is the mathematical symbol of ratio. 3 cm. 2 cm. 2 cm. 3 cm. In the above figures, we see that there are two squares of areas 4 sq. cm and . _ 4 9 9 sq. cm. The ratio of areas of the squares is ra 4:9 or 47 9:4 depending on which comes first. We see that ratio is a fraction. Let us observe the following examples : (a) The rectangular region is divided into 7 equal parts, 2 parts are white and the remaining 5 parts are black. The ratio of white and black parts is 2: 5 and here 2 and 5 are called antecedent and consequent respectively. (b) Showkat’s weight is 30 kg and that of his father is 60 kg. How many times is the weight of Showkats father greater than that of his? Ratio of the weights of father and Showkat is 60 kg 2 oo. 30 ke = 1 [Dividing the numerator & denominator by 30] =2:1 Here, the weight of father is twice the weight of Showkat. (c) Inaclass, there are 50 boys and 40 girls Here, the ratio of the number of boys and girls is 2026 2026 Mathematics 47 [dividing the numerator & denominator by 10] = 5:4 Is it possible to compare the age of a child to the weight of a child ? It can never be done. To compare, two objects should be of the same kind.',557,'06-Mathematics.pdf','tesseract','tesseract 5.3.4 -l eng, pdftoppm 200dpi gray; verbatim OCR, whitespace collapsed only; ~50-word overlap with previous chunk in same chapter; first chunk of chapter, no overlap; maths notation may OCR noisily, see extraction log'),
('class6-math-en-chunk-2-002','class6-math-en','class6-math-en-ch2','en',46,49,51,54,2,'of boys and girls is 2026 2026 Mathematics 47 [dividing the numerator & denominator by 10] = 5:4 Is it possible to compare the age of a child to the weight of a child ? It can never be done. To compare, two objects should be of the same kind. Further, let us suppose that the age of a baby is 6 years and that of another baby is 9 years 6 months. Though the ages are of the same kind, they can not be compared directly. The two objects of comparison should be of the same unit. In this case, the age of two babies should be expressed either in years or in months. Here, 6 years = (6 x 12) months = 72 months (-.* 1 year = 12 months) 9 years 6 months = (9 x 12 + 6) months = 114 months. So, the ratio of age of two babies is 72: 114 or 12:19. Let us consider that the age of brother is 3 years and that of sister is 6 months. We shall have to find the ratio of their ages. Brother’s age = 3 years =3 x 12 months (-,; 1 year = 12 months) = 36 months Sister’s age = 6 months .. Ratio of their ages th = 36 months ore or? [Dividing numerator and denominator by 6] 6 months 6 =6:1 Observe: It is to be noted that the different units can not be compared with each other. To compare, the units are to be of the same kind. Such as, the year has been converted into months in the above example. Of two quantities of the same kind one is how many times or parts compared to the other can be expressed by fraction. This fraction is called ratio of two quantities. Since the two quantities are of the same kind, the ratio has no unit. Activity : 1. Determine the ratio of numbers of your khatas and books. 2. Determine the ratio of breadth and lengthof your math book. 3. Determine the ratio of length and breadth of the table of your class. 48 Mathematics 2:2 Different Ratios Equivalent Ratios Thevalue of a ratio remains unchanged when its antecedent and consequent are multiplied or divided by any number except 0. Such ratios are called equivalent ratio. “. 2:5 and 4: 10 are equivalent ratios. There are an infinite number of equivalent ratios of any ratio. Such as, 2:3,4:6,6:9and 8: 12 are all equivalent ratios. Again, in 1 : 2 = 5 :[_], if we put 10 in the blanks, the ratio will be an equivalent ratio. © Observe : > The ratio can be simplified if the terms of the ratio are divided by H.C.F. (Highest Common Factor). > Each part of the ratio can be found if they are divided by the sum of the antecedent and consequent of the ratio. Example 1: The present age ratio of Jesmin and Abida is 3:2. The present age ratio of Abida and Anika is 5:1. The present age of Anika is 3 years 6 months. (a) Express the first ratio in the stem into percentage. (b) What will be Abida''s age after 5 years? (c) What is the percentage of Jesmin''s present age to Anika''s present age? Solution: (a) The first ratio of the stem = 3.2 3 a) _ 3x 100 ~ 2x 100 _ (3x 100 = (EM) % = 150% 2026 Mathematics 49 (b) Present age of Abida: present age of Anika =5:1.It means Abida’s present age is 5 times more than Anika’s.',597,'06-Mathematics.pdf','tesseract','tesseract 5.3.4 -l eng, pdftoppm 200dpi gray; verbatim OCR, whitespace collapsed only; ~50-word overlap with previous chunk in same chapter; maths notation may OCR noisily, see extraction log'),
