('class6-math-en-chunk-4-007','class6-math-en','class6-math-en-ch4','en',100,102,105,107,7,'mx mm? xn xm xn! (v) 3ax 4bx 2a x 5c x 3b (vi) 2x* x y? x 22? x3y? x 4x? 2. Ifa=2,b=3,c=1, find the value of each of the following expressions : (i) a +b° (ii) +e (iii) @ —P +0 (iv) b° —2ab+a° (v) a@ —2ac+e 3. If x=3,y=5,z=2, show that (i) y? -x* =(x+ y(y-x) (ii) (x+y) =(x— yp)’ + 4xy (iii) (yt+z)y =y+2yz4+27 (iv) (x+z) =x? +2xz4+27 4. Tick the correct answers : (i) Which one is the value of a’ xa* ? (a) a®° (b) a” (c) 15 (d) 56 (ii) Which one is the value of a* x a™ ? (a) a® (b) a (c) a® (d) a’ 2026 2026 Mathematics 101 (iii) Which one is the value of 5x” x 4x* ? (a) x° (b) 20x° — (c) 20x (d) 9x° (iv) Which one is the index of x in x° x x* ? (a) x?" (b) x? (c) 9 (d) 20 (v) Which one is the index of a in Sa* xa’ ? (a) 5 (b) a’ (c) 15 (d) 8 4-4 Similar and dissimilar terms - Ta’bx, 8a°bx are two algebraic expressions. The difference between two the terms of expressions is only in their numerical coefficients. These two terms are similar terms. Two terms are called similar, if they differ only in their numerical coefficients. Otherwise, the terms are called dissimilar. For Example, the numerical coefficient of two expressions 9ax and Yay is the same, but two terms are different, so they are dissimilar. Similar and dissimilar terms can be observed in the following examples : Similar terms: (i) 5a, 6a (ii) 3a’, Sa’ (iii) Sabx, 8xab (iv) 2x*ab, —x°ab (v) 3x° yz, Syx?z, Tyzx? Dissimilar terms: (i) 3xy’, 3x’y (ii) Sabx, Saby (iii) ax*y?, bx*y7z, cxy’ (iv) ax’ yz, bxy’z, cxyz Observe: Even if the algebraic symbols in more than one term are the same and their numerical coefficients are equal, they may be dissimi- lar. Such as, 3ax? and 3x’a are similar terms, but Sab? and 5a’b are dis- similar terms. 2 4:5 Addition of the algebraic expressions For addition of two or more algebraic expressions, the coefficients of the similar terms are to be added by the rule of addition of signed numbers, then the symbols are to be placed next to the coefficient obtained. Dissimilar terms with their signs are to be placed in the total. 102 Example 12 (a). Add: 2a+4b+5c,3a+2b-6c. Solution: (2a+ 4b +5c)+(3a+2b-6c) = (2a +3a)+ (4b + 2b) + (Sc — 6c) = 5a+6b-c. The required sum is 5a + 6b -c. Alternative method : First write the similar terms with their signs, one below the other, Now we get, 2a+4b+5c + 3a+2b-—6c 5a+6b-c The required sum is 5a + 6b - c. Mathematics Example 12 (b). Add : 3a+6b+c,5a+2b+d. Solution : (3a+6b+c)+(5a+2b+d) = Bat+5a)+(6b+2b)+c+d = 8a+8b+c+d. [Here, by adding the similar terms that have been added with the sum of dissimilar terms] The required sum is 8a+ 8b+c+d. Observe : The algebraic sum of the numerical coefficients of the similar terms has been determined. Algebraic symbols are to be placed next to the obtained sum. Then the sum of all terms obtained is the required sum. Example 13. Add: 5a+3b—c’, —3a+4b+4c’, a—8b+2c’. Solution : Arranging the similar terms one below the other, we get, Sa+3b- ¢?',555,'06-Mathematics.pdf','tesseract','tesseract 5.3.4 -l eng, pdftoppm 200dpi gray; verbatim OCR, whitespace collapsed only; ~50-word overlap with previous chunk in same chapter; maths notation may OCR noisily, see extraction log'),
